course: dictionary
lesson: 8957
title: Similes: comparing with like or as
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: A simile is a way of describing something by comparing it to something else. It joins the two things with the word «like» or the word «as». «As brave as a lion» tells you someone is very brave, because a lion is brave, so you picture the bravery.
explain: Similes help the reader picture something clearly and feel it more strongly. «She ran like the wind» makes you see how fast she moved. William Wordsworth wrote «I wandered lonely as a cloud», comparing himself to a cloud drifting along all alone.
explain: To spot a simile, look for «like» or «as» joining two different things. A metaphor is different: it says one thing IS another, such as «The sun is a golden coin». A few strong similes beat a crowd of weak ones, so choose yours carefully.
fact: A simile compares two things using «like» or «as».
fact: «As cold as ice» is a simile: it tells you something is very cold.
fact: «Like a fish out of water» means someone feels out of place.
fact: A metaphor says one thing IS another, while a simile says it is like or as another thing.
fact: In stories, a fresh simile is better than a tired one.
exercise: Which sentence is a simile? | «The wind was loud.» | «The wind is a wild horse.» | «The wind howled all night.» | *«She ran like the wind.»
exercise: Which word joins the two things in a simile? | sun | coin | *like | golden
exercise: «The sun is a golden coin.» Is this a simile or a metaphor? | Neither, it is just a fact about the sun | Simile, because it is about the sun | Simile, because it has the word golden in it | *Metaphor, because it says the sun IS a coin
test: Which phrase is a simile for someone very brave? | lions are brave animals | *as brave as a lion | brave is a lion | a brave lion roared
test: Why do similes help readers? | They stop readers reading on | They make a poem longer and harder to read | *They help readers picture something and feel it more strongly | They are only used in maths lessons
exam: In «The wind's screamin' through the streets, like», is the word like a simile? | Yes, because it contains the word like | *No, it is just Scouse filler, so the trick there is personification | No, because the sentence is a metaphor | Yes, because the wind is compared to something
